WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Film Courage: Travis I’m wondering if we&nbsp;
can talk about the levels of dimension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.480
in a character? So if we can talk about what&nbsp;
a three-dimensional character looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.600
and then one that is two-dimensional and&nbsp;
finally just a one-dimensional characters?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.920
How does one look flat versus a fully&nbsp;
fleshed out three-dimensional character?

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.160
Travis Seppala, Author/Screenwriter:&nbsp;
I’m not sure how to do 2-D? That’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.440
interesting concept…1-D and 3-D make sense. 2-D…?

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.480
Film Courage: I created that.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.800
Travis: Okay. I guess 2-D is telling&nbsp;
me a piece of paper, so there is that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.840
A single dimensional character is somebody that&nbsp;
is just kind of there to facilitate the plot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:48.320
They are there to just basically be a moving piece&nbsp;
along the way where they are just an anchor for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.840
the story. They are usually somebody who the story&nbsp;
is happening to and they are not really making any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.840
choices, they are just kind of being pushed along&nbsp;
with all the stuff that is occurring around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.840
a three-dimensional character is more like a&nbsp;
real human being who makes choices and some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.320
those choices are good and some of those choices&nbsp;
are bad. There are somebody who actually makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:15.863
decisions about thing. There are not somebody&nbsp;
who is being pushed along by if the bad person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.863 --> 00:01:21.440
is running after them they are not just going&nbsp;
upstairs because that is where the story needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.920
take place. They are somebody who might actually&nbsp;
fight back against the bad guy or somebody who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:36.160
may go cower in a closet somewhere. It’s very&nbsp;
character specific. Somebody who may have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:44.800
different experience. Have the exact same story&nbsp;
and you have the main character be a hairdresser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.520
they are going to make way different decisions&nbsp;
than a character who is a FBI agent and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.080
would be a three-dimensional character because&nbsp;
they are all the choices of what they are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:01.440
has to do with their experiences. 1D character&nbsp;
would be we don’t…if it was an FBI agent or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.640
beauty salon person…it doesn’t matter because&nbsp;
they’re just going where the story is going. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.920
that’s my best. Again, 2D is a piece of paper. I&nbsp;
don’t know what to do with that for a character.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.640
Film Courage: Or maybe they are an FBI agent&nbsp;
that has a cover as a hair salon [owner] and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.800
then these woman confide all of these things and&nbsp;
they are like “Ah!” and then they get these tips.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.640
Are there different tests though that&nbsp;
when you look at the characters that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.640
you’re writing and you say “You know what,&nbsp;
this is two one-dimensional right here.”

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:43.200
Travis: Well I try to come up with…I am&nbsp;
personally more of a plot writer than a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:50.640
writer. But, even in having the plot you need the&nbsp;
characters to make sense. I still need to find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:57.280
character to have in this place. If I want an&nbsp;
alien invasion story I am not going to write a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.840
character who could be anything just because all&nbsp;
I care about is the cool explosions that having an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:09.600
alien invasion happen so I need to find characters&nbsp;
that are going to make cool decisions that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.720
cinematic based on what’s happening to them.&nbsp;
So if I want something that is going to be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:21.200
high impact action where the good guy is fighting&nbsp;
back, I might have a soldier as the lead character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.960
and they are going to make way different&nbsp;
decisions than just the general public is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.360
They are going to be helping other people get out&nbsp;
the situation. They’re going to be fighting back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.680
against the aliens. They are going to be plotting&nbsp;
and scheming and coming up with whatever they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.200
to survive, help others survive and defeat the&nbsp;
bad guy. Whereas if I want something that is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.200
a terror driven thing where we are on the edge of&nbsp;
our seats “Oh my gosh! When is this person going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.720
to go?” I might have just a regular Joe Schmoe&nbsp;
who has no experience, they’re just looking out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.440
for themselves, maybe they are not helping other&nbsp;
people unless they can’t help it who is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:04.000
be cowering in their car and they are probably&nbsp;
going to go at any point. So at that point it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.960
like so when is this person going to go versus the&nbsp;
soldier - Oh! When is he going to beat the aliens?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.400
Because it’s the same setup because it’s a&nbsp;
different character it’s a completely different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:21.760
story. It’s kind of one of those things of I want&nbsp;
this story to be about a specific…to go a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:28.080
route I need to find the character that helps that&nbsp;
route along and it isn’t just some random person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.960
based on what the experiences are and even&nbsp;
then my story because again I’m outlining so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.880
in the outline I am coming up with what character&nbsp;
would best facilitate it now I have to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.000
what decisions is this character going to make&nbsp;
and how does this change the rest of the plot that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.680
I originally had in mind and so going from my&nbsp;
initial one sentence thing that I write in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.480
idea book maybe it can be a completely different&nbsp;
animal now that I have the character for it.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:58.640
Film Courage: If we go back to 2005&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.429
which you said was loosely based&nbsp;
on you or it was really your story?

00:05:01.429 --> 00:05:05.760
Travis: Right, it was a loosely based thing,&nbsp;
like the character was going through the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.240
stuff that I was going through,&nbsp;
sometimes a little more dramatic than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.400
what my own stuff was and a vastly different&nbsp;
outcome from what my personal experience was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.640
but yes, that script was loosely&nbsp;
based on the situation I was in.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.840
Film Courage: Do you feel that that&nbsp;
character was three-dimensional?

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.240
Travis: I feel like that character was&nbsp;
me in a sense and me kind of living out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.880
my own fantasy of how what I feel like I&nbsp;
should have done. The first two-thirds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:43.040
that script was the character was a weak as&nbsp;
I was in this situation where he was letting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:50.320
this stuff happen to him and not really fighting&nbsp;
back and I felt like the stuff I was writing was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.720
basically like how I wanted to get out of&nbsp;
that situation but in real life I was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:02.560
too weak to do it. I knew what needed to be done,&nbsp;
I just couldn’t convince myself to actually do it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:08.240
So I had the character who had the same situation&nbsp;
as me and he was just as weak as I was until he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.520
finally found the strength to push forward and do&nbsp;
what needed to be done and even then once I wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:20.800
that point that was when I was able to like end&nbsp;
that scenario in my own life. I was in an abusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:27.680
relationship as is what that was and so it was&nbsp;
almost like a reverse Lifetime movie you where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.160
a lot of Lifetime movies where you’ve got the&nbsp;
woman who is being beaten by the man and stuff and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.800
following her trail and so it was the guy being&nbsp;
beaten by his wife at the time you know and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.840
in real life I couldn’t get myself to get out&nbsp;
of that situation. I’m about to cry, I’m sorry.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:45.840
Film Courage: I’m sorry. I feel bad now.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.560
Travis: No worries. So I knew I needed&nbsp;
to get out of that situation but I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:55.360
couldn’t get myself to do it because I was&nbsp;
like low self-esteem at the time so I felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:04.480
like I couldn’t do any better than that. So&nbsp;
in the writing he rose above it…I’m sorry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:13.760
So in the story he found the power that I&nbsp;
couldn’t basically and rose above it and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.840
had a much, much different ending obviously&nbsp;
than just leaving because I wanted to be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.760
kind of cinematic too so there was some&nbsp;
thrillery stuff thrown into the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:29.280
act of that obviously never happened in&nbsp;
real life but it was the same situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:34.160
the same sort of feelings, and so that’s why it&nbsp;
was kind of like therapy for me to get out of it.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:43.840
Question For The Viewers: Anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.343
you can add on creating&nbsp;
three-dimensional characters?

